SECTION D — PARITY NOTES (web source is THE specification)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
D1. States reproduced are exactly those the web source has — it reads SYNCHRONOUS router state (useLocation) +
    document.title; there is NO fetch/query/cache/connectivity, so there is NO loading/error/stale/offline
    branch to reproduce. Inventing them would drift from the spec. The real states are all rendered:
      idle (initial route → suppressed, region empty, silent),
      announcing (route change → region carries title + rotating 0–3 ZWSP padding, VoiceOver speaks it),
      cleared (settled title empty → region cleared, silent).
D2. Data sources: bound to the navigation feed (native useLocation peer = RouteTitleSource). The web source
    DELIBERATELY does not route through useAnnouncer (it documents why — route changes are declarative and need
    their own region); the native port mirrors that by delivering through its own RouteAnnouncing port rather
    than a shared announcer holder. The prompt's auto-listed "useAnnouncer" is superseded by the actual source.
D3. Apple idiom: the web `<VisuallyHidden liveRegion priority="polite">` maps to
    AccessibilityNotification.Announcement at .default (polite) speech priority — the sanctioned route-change
    idiom (ADR-009), already used inline by Sources/App/AppShell.handle(); this surface extracts a reusable,
    tested component. The hidden region view is zero-size + accessibilityHidden so it is never double-spoken.
D4. i18n (P1/S10): table "RouteAnnouncer", no English prose literal in code. Telemetry (P1/S11): view.opened
    emitted once with surface slug "RouteAnnouncer". Accessibility: no interactive elements (a hidden announcer);
    Dynamic Type semantic font, reduce-motion respected (no animation), localized region label asserted by test.

